{0 Migration Guide}

This guide helps you migrate to [ocaml_telegram_eio] from other Telegram bot libraries or from traditional callback-based OCaml code to Eio's direct-style concurrency.

## Table of Contents

- [Migration from telegram-bot-simple (Haskell-inspired)](#migration-from-telegram-bot-simple)
- [Migration from Lwt/Async to Eio](#migration-from-lwtasync-to-eio)
- [Migration from synchronous OCaml code](#migration-from-synchronous-ocaml-code)
- [Migrating from raw Telegram Bot API calls](#migrating-from-raw-telegram-bot-api-calls)
- [Key differences and design decisions](#key-differences-and-design-decisions)

---

## Migration from telegram-bot-simple

If you're coming from Haskell's `telegram-bot-simple` or similar declarative bot frameworks, you'll find familiar patterns in `ocaml-telegram-eio`.

### Basic bot structure

**Before (conceptual Haskell-like):**
```haskell
bot :: BotApp Model Action
bot = BotApp
  { botInitialModel = ()
  , botAction = updateToAction
  , botHandler = handleAction
  , botJobs = []
  }
```

**After (ocaml-telegram-eio):**
```ocaml
let () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  let client = Telegram.Client.create ~env ~token () in
  let handler update =
    (* Handle updates *)
    match update.Update.message with
    | Some msg -> handle_message client msg
    | None -> ()
  in
  Tg.Polling.run client ~handler
```

### Command handling

**Before:**
```haskell
handleAction :: Action -> Model -> Eff Action Model
handleAction action model = case action of
  Help -> model <# do
    reply "Available commands: /start, /help"
  Start -> ...
```

**After:**
```ocaml
let handle_update update =
  let open Telegram_generated.Gen_types in
  match update.Update.message with
  | Some msg ->
      let chat_id = Telegram.Id.Chat.of_int msg.Message.chat.Chat.id in
      let text = Option.value msg.Message.text ~default:"" in

      (match parse_command text with
       | Some "/start" ->
           let* _ = Gen_methods.send_message client ~chat_id ~text:"Welcome!" () in
           Ok ()
       | Some "/help" ->
           let* _ = Gen_methods.send_message client ~chat_id ~text:"Available commands..." () in
           Ok ()
       | _ -> Ok ())
  | None -> Ok ()
```

### Keyboard creation

**Before:**
```haskell
replyKeyboardMarkup [["Button 1", "Button 2"], ["Button 3"]]
```

**After:**
```ocaml
let keyboard = Keyboard.reply [
  ["Button 1"; "Button 2"];
  ["Button 3"];
] in
let reply_markup = Yojson.Safe.to_string keyboard in
Gen_methods.send_message client ~chat_id ~text:"Choose:" ~reply_markup ()
```

### Inline keyboards with callbacks

**Before:**
```haskell
inlineKeyboardMarkup [[callbackButton "Click me" "callback_data"]]
```

**After:**
```ocaml
let buttons = [
  [Keyboard.callback ~text:"Click me" ~data:"callback_data"];
] in
let reply_markup = Keyboard.inline buttons in
Gen_methods.send_message client ~chat_id ~text:"Click:" ~reply_markup ()
```

